  "id": "Compound::DB00679 - Gene::3356",
  "verbalization": "Thioridazine, a known antipsychotic agent, is implicated in the relationship with Htr2a through a series of mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Thioridazine may inhibit Htr2a through binding, as multiple paths indicate that Thioridazine causes effects that are mediated by Htr2a. Specifically, Thioridazine is linked to pregnancy test false positives, which are caused by Zuclopenthixol or Chlorpromazine, both of which bind to Htr2a. These findings suggest a potential role for Htr2a in the pathophysiology of the effects observed when Thioridazine is administered. The shared ancestry of Thioridazine with antipsychotic agents and organonitrogen compounds further supports a common biological pathway. The integration of these findings implies that Thioridazine may exert its effects via a mechanism involving Htr2a, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
